    "created_at": "2025-05-14T01:07:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-05-14T01:07:38Z",
    "body": "I think the main point here is the functionality role/character differences for dialogs and popovers? For the properties, dialogs are supposed to serve some functions, like date selection, inputs, forms, etc., but for popovers, they tend to include more simple contents, as just \"popovers\". Whether they should be modal or non-modal depend on their functionality properties, meaning that how important they are, like is it critical or requiring immediate attention?\n\nNon-modal dialogs could just be triggered by just `open` attributes, meaning their layout is simpler, differing from popovers appended to top layers. However, there would be another topic that while the new popover API and the old non-modal dialogs exist for similar mechanisms, their advance of supports and functionalities may render they may either be unified or combined.\n\nAbout whether they should be in the top layer, it should be that, are they important to be \"always on top\"? If they do not serve functional uses, they are probably not dialogs, but simple alerts or just \"popovers\"/\"popups\"; otherwise they should be something on top, requiring high priority of attention and user's action. Still, having a non-top dialogs may not be a practical designs for websites, while they are not advanced like OS windows, but locked within a window.\n\nUnless we simplify the situation to use either dialogs or popovers, there might be some ambiguous differences between their APIs. However, `popover` is an attribute feature, which means every element could actually be a popover, but it may not make much sense when dialogs are actual elements. They are distinct APIs, so they could neither be well unified (unless by recommendation) or combined, even making one deprecated.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
